EXT. MAIL-ORDER WINDMILL RANCH - DAY

Wind pumps clank. Cattle low. A porch looks out on a dry creek.

MAGDALENA "MAGGIE" SOTO, 40s, sits on the porch rail rolling a cigarette. Her daughter, ROSALIND SOTO, 20s, mends a bridle. A drifter, COLTON PIKE, 30s, stands at the gate with his hat in his hands. His horse, a sorry mare, waits behind him. A young ranch hand, BEAU TILLMAN, 18, stacks hay bales nearby.

COLTON
Ma'am, I heard you're hiring.

MAGGIE
Heard from who?

COLTON
Gas station.

MAGGIE
We don't have a gas station.

COLTON
I made a wrong turn, then.

Rosalind snorts. Beau drops a bale on his toe.

BEAU
(hopping)
Ow!

ROSALIND
Beau.

BEAU
I'm fine.

MAGGIE
Can you ride, Mr. ...?

COLTON
Pike. Colton Pike. I can ride, rope, shoe, fix a windmill.

ROSALIND
Can you cook?

COLTON
Badly.

MAGGIE
We've got a cook.

BEAU
We've got a Rosalind.

ROSALIND
He means me.

MAGGIE
Rosalind, show him the bunkhouse.

COLTON
Thank you, ma'am.

Rosalind sets the bridle down and heads off. Colton follows. Maggie lights her cigarette.

MAGGIE
Beau?

BEAU
Yes, ma'am?

MAGGIE
Wires on the south fence were cut last night. Watch him.

BEAU
The drifter?

MAGGIE
The drifter.

Beau nods, swallows, and picks up another bale. Maggie exhales smoke and watches Colton cross the yard, the mare nosing at his shoulder.

EXT. BUNKHOUSE - CONTINUOUS

Rosalind pushes open the door. Dust. Cots.

ROSALIND
Mom likes you.

COLTON
She hides it well.

ROSALIND
She hides everything well.

Colton sets his saddlebag on a cot, pauses, and runs a thumb along the wire cutters poking out of the flap. He tucks them deeper.

MAGGIE (O.S.)
(calling)
Rosalind, supper's at six!

ROSALIND
(calling back)
Yes, Mom!

Colton and Rosalind exchange a glance. Hers is curious. His is careful.
